{"reason": "The initial plan proposes replacing the 3-branch MLP with a Transformer encoder to capture cross-feature interactions, and adding cosine annealing scheduler. These require only model architecture and training strategy changes; the feature extraction pipeline remains effective. Historical experiments showed that CANINE transformer integration failed due to architecture mismatch and import errors, but using pre-extracted features with a simple neural network succeeded. Thus, modifying 'model_design' and 'training_evaluation' leverages robust features and avoids pitfalls of full text-based transformers. Feature engineering is unchanged as it already produces good inputs.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the current 3-branch MLP with a Transformer encoder that takes concatenated dense (45-dim), semantic (384-dim), and sparse (13500-dim) features as input. Use a single linear projection to reduce the total dimension to 256, then pass through 4 Transformer encoder layers with 4 attention heads, feedforward dimension 512, and dropout 0.3. Apply global average pooling over the sequence dimension (add a learned [CLS] token or pool over tokens) and a final linear classification head to 3 classes. This change directly implements the initial plan's architectural deepening: self-attention captures cross-feature interactions among stylometric, semantic, and n-gram features. Keep input normalization (BatchNorm before projection) as in the current code.", "training_evaluation": "Replace the linear scheduler with CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts with T_0=10 (first restart after 10 epochs), T_mult=2, and keep other hyperparameters (AdamW, weight decay 0.01, batch size 32, label smoothing 0.1, patience 7). This change follows the initial plan's scheduler modification to handle deeper model convergence. Due to historical CANINE integration failures, do not alter the data loaders or feature pipelines—they remain as is. The training loop and evaluation metric (log loss) stay unchanged. Also, add mixed-precision training (GradScaler and autocast) to speed up training and reduce memory, which was not mentioned originally but will help with the larger model."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan proposes replacing the 3-branch MLP with a Transformer encoder to capture cross-feature interactions, and adding cosine annealing scheduler. These require only model architecture and training strategy changes; the feature extraction pipeline remains effective. Historical experiments showed that CANINE transformer integration failed due to architecture mismatch and import errors, but using pre-extracted features with a simple neural network succeeded. Thus, modifying 'model_design' and 'training_evaluation' leverages robust features and avoids pitfalls of full text-based transformers. Feature engineering is unchanged as it already produces good inputs.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the current 3-branch MLP with a Transformer encoder that takes concatenated dense (45-dim), semantic (384-dim), and sparse (13500-dim) features as input. Use a single linear projection to reduce the total dimension to 256, then pass through 4 Transformer encoder layers with 4 attention heads, feedforward dimension 512, and dropout 0.3. Apply global average pooling over the sequence dimension (add a learned [CLS] token or pool over tokens) and a final linear classification head to 3 classes. This change directly implements the initial plan's architectural deepening: self-attention captures cross-feature interactions among stylometric, semantic, and n-gram features. Keep input normalization (BatchNorm before projection) as in the current code.",
    "training_evaluation": "Replace the linear scheduler with CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts with T_0=10 (first restart after 10 epochs), T_mult=2, and keep other hyperparameters (AdamW, weight decay 0.01, batch size 32, label smoothing 0.1, patience 7). This change follows the initial plan's scheduler modification to handle deeper model convergence. Due to historical CANINE integration failures, do not alter the data loaders or feature pipelines—they remain as is. The training loop and evaluation metric (log loss) stay unchanged. Also, add mixed-precision training (GradScaler and autocast) to speed up training and reduce memory, which was not mentioned originally but will help with the larger model."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan focuses on adding cross-attention fusion and differentiated learning rates to better utilize semantic embeddings. Historical experiments show that similar feature-rich architectures with transformer fusion can improve log loss from ~0.85 to ~0.70-0.75, but a simpler MLP approach (as in the current code) achieved ~0.85 validation log loss. The current transformer encoder with stacked tokens underperforms because it treats all three modalities equally. Therefore, I refine the model_design to implement cross-attention and differentiated LRs, and also refine training_evaluation to adjust optimizer parameter groups. Data processing remains unchanged as features are already well-engineered.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "1) Replace the simple stack-and-pool transformer encoder with a Cross-Modal Attention (CMA) fusion block: use semantic embeddings as query (Q) and concatenated dense+sparse features as key/value (K/V) in a multi-head attention layer, followed by residual connection, layer normalization, and a lightweight MLP head. 2) Keep the three projection heads (proj_dense, proj_sem, proj_sparse) but output to the same d_model dimension (256). 3) The forward method will compute cross-attention between the semantic token and the dense/sparse token, then classify via a small MLP. This change exploits the natural inductive bias that semantic context should query stylometric patterns, similar to successful historical experiments that used attention for modality fusion.", "training_evaluation": "1) Implement differentiated learning rates: separate optimizer parameter groups for the semantic projection branch (lr=5e-3) and the dense/sparse projection branches plus the CMA fusion and classifier (lr=5e-4). Use AdamW with weight_decay=0.01. 2) Keep all other training hyperparameters unchanged (batch size 32, epochs 50, warmup ratio 0.1, gradient clipping, label smoothing, early stopping). 3) This differential LR mimics fine-tuning strategies that worked in historical experiments, allowing the semantic branch to adapt quickly while preserving handcrafted features."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan focuses on adding cross-attention fusion and differentiated learning rates to better utilize semantic embeddings. Historical experiments show that similar feature-rich architectures with transformer fusion can improve log loss from ~0.85 to ~0.70-0.75, but a simpler MLP approach (as in the current code) achieved ~0.85 validation log loss. The current transformer encoder with stacked tokens underperforms because it treats all three modalities equally. Therefore, I refine the model_design to implement cross-attention and differentiated LRs, and also refine training_evaluation to adjust optimizer parameter groups. Data processing remains unchanged as features are already well-engineered.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "1) Replace the simple stack-and-pool transformer encoder with a Cross-Modal Attention (CMA) fusion block: use semantic embeddings as query (Q) and concatenated dense+sparse features as key/value (K/V) in a multi-head attention layer, followed by residual connection, layer normalization, and a lightweight MLP head. 2) Keep the three projection heads (proj_dense, proj_sem, proj_sparse) but output to the same d_model dimension (256). 3) The forward method will compute cross-attention between the semantic token and the dense/sparse token, then classify via a small MLP. This change exploits the natural inductive bias that semantic context should query stylometric patterns, similar to successful historical experiments that used attention for modality fusion.",
    "training_evaluation": "1) Implement differentiated learning rates: separate optimizer parameter groups for the semantic projection branch (lr=5e-3) and the dense/sparse projection branches plus the CMA fusion and classifier (lr=5e-4). Use AdamW with weight_decay=0.01. 2) Keep all other training hyperparameters unchanged (batch size 32, epochs 50, warmup ratio 0.1, gradient clipping, label smoothing, early stopping). 3) This differential LR mimics fine-tuning strategies that worked in historical experiments, allowing the semantic branch to adapt quickly while preserving handcrafted features."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan's core direction is to replace the handcrafted feature pipeline with a fine-tuned DeBERTa-v3-base transformer model for end-to-end author classification. Historical experiments show that feature-rich architectures with cross-attention fusion can improve log loss, but they still rely on handcrafted features, which are inferior to a fine-tuned pretrained language model. The current implementation already includes some of those improvements (cross-attention, differentiated LRs) but still uses pre-extracted features, limiting performance. Therefore, the refinement focuses on data_processing_and_feature_engineering (to stream to raw text for the new model) and model_design (to implement the DeBERTa-v3-base transformer), keeping training_evaluation mostly unchanged (to reuse proven training loop, loss, scheduler, and early stopping).", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Replace the multi-feature engineering pipeline (stylometric, readability, POS, n-grams, semantic embeddings, etc.) with a minimal preprocessing step that only tokenizes raw text for the DeBERTa-v3 tokenizer. Specifically, remove all feature extraction functions and scalers. Keep the stratified train-validation split and label encoding. Store raw text in the dataset instead of precomputed features. This change is necessary because the initial plan's core is to drop handcrafted features and use a fine-tuned transformer; historical experiments confirm that even with cross-attention fusion, handcrafted features plateau compared to transformer-based representations. Implementation: modify the data loading to only keep X_train_texts, X_val_texts, y_train_labels, y_val_labels as raw strings and labels; remove all code for train_dense, train_semantic_emb, train_sparse, etc. The DataLoader will return text strings and labels, tokenized on the fly inside the model forward pass.", "model_design": "Replace the CrossModalAttentionFusion model with a fine-tuned DeBERTa-v3-base transformer for sequence classification. Use `AutoModelForSequenceClassification.from_pretrained('microsoft/deberta-v3-base', num_labels=3)` and add a dropout layer before the classifier head. The forward method will tokenize the raw text using the DeBERTa tokenizer (with padding, truncation, max_length=512) and return logits. Keep the same loss function (CrossEntropyLoss with label_smoothing=0.1), optimizer (AdamW), and learning rate schedule as in the initial plan (lr=2e-5 for all parameters, weight_decay=0.01, warmup ratio=0.1). This change aligns with the initial plan's direction to use a fine-tuned transformer; historical experiments show that DeBERTa-v3 significantly outperforms feature-based methods on author identification. Implementation: define a new class `DebertaAuthorClassifier` that loads the pretrained model and a tokenizer. Remove the old model class entirely. The model will directly take text strings as input, tokenize them, and compute logits without any handcrafted features."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan's core direction is to replace the handcrafted feature pipeline with a fine-tuned DeBERTa-v3-base transformer model for end-to-end author classification. Historical experiments show that feature-rich architectures with cross-attention fusion can improve log loss, but they still rely on handcrafted features, which are inferior to a fine-tuned pretrained language model. The current implementation already includes some of those improvements (cross-attention, differentiated LRs) but still uses pre-extracted features, limiting performance. Therefore, the refinement focuses on data_processing_and_feature_engineering (to stream to raw text for the new model) and model_design (to implement the DeBERTa-v3-base transformer), keeping training_evaluation mostly unchanged (to reuse proven training loop, loss, scheduler, and early stopping).",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "model_design"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Replace the multi-feature engineering pipeline (stylometric, readability, POS, n-grams, semantic embeddings, etc.) with a minimal preprocessing step that only tokenizes raw text for the DeBERTa-v3 tokenizer. Specifically, remove all feature extraction functions and scalers. Keep the stratified train-validation split and label encoding. Store raw text in the dataset instead of precomputed features. This change is necessary because the initial plan's core is to drop handcrafted features and use a fine-tuned transformer; historical experiments confirm that even with cross-attention fusion, handcrafted features plateau compared to transformer-based representations. Implementation: modify the data loading to only keep X_train_texts, X_val_texts, y_train_labels, y_val_labels as raw strings and labels; remove all code for train_dense, train_semantic_emb, train_sparse, etc. The DataLoader will return text strings and labels, tokenized on the fly inside the model forward pass.",
    "model_design": "Replace the CrossModalAttentionFusion model with a fine-tuned DeBERTa-v3-base transformer for sequence classification. Use `AutoModelForSequenceClassification.from_pretrained('microsoft/deberta-v3-base', num_labels=3)` and add a dropout layer before the classifier head. The forward method will tokenize the raw text using the DeBERTa tokenizer (with padding, truncation, max_length=512) and return logits. Keep the same loss function (CrossEntropyLoss with label_smoothing=0.1), optimizer (AdamW), and learning rate schedule as in the initial plan (lr=2e-5 for all parameters, weight_decay=0.01, warmup ratio=0.1). This change aligns with the initial plan's direction to use a fine-tuned transformer; historical experiments show that DeBERTa-v3 significantly outperforms feature-based methods on author identification. Implementation: define a new class `DebertaAuthorClassifier` that loads the pretrained model and a tokenizer. Remove the old model class entirely. The model will directly take text strings as input, tokenize them, and compute logits without any handcrafted features."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan proposes three changes: multi-fold cross-validation, tokenizer parallelism fixes, and gradient accumulation. Execution results show that validation loss oscillates wildly (e.g., 0.5059 at epoch 10, 0.8604 at epoch 11) and repeated tokenizer warnings occur, confirming the issues. Historical successful experiments used a DeBERTa-v3 transformer with raw text and a single train/val split, achieving reasonable performance but left room for improvement. The failures in historical experiments (e.g., feature engineering with dimension mismatch) reinforce the need for focus on data processing and training strategy. Therefore, refinements should target data_processing_and_feature_engineering to fix tokenizer warnings, training_evaluation to implement multi-fold CV and gradient accumulation, while model_design remains unchanged (DeBERTa-v3 is already correct).", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Add `os.environ['TOKENIZERS_PARALLELISM']='false'` at the top of the script before any tokenizer imports, and move tokenization inside the DataLoader worker initialization by using a custom Dataset that tokenizes in __getitem__ (not in collate_fn) to avoid fork-related deadlocks. WHY: The execution shows repeated tokenizer parallelism warnings, which can cause data loading corruption and silent performance degradation. Historical experiments confirm that stable tokenization is critical for reproducibility. HOW: Insert environment variable setting right after path config and before any transformers import; modify TextDataset to tokenize in __getitem__ and store tokenized outputs, or use a custom collate_fn that tokenizes within the worker process. Interface constraints: preserve existing dataset class name and method signatures, but adapt internal implementation.", "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Replace the single 80/20 split with 5-fold StratifiedKFold cross-validation (as already set up but only first fold used) and average predictions from all 5 models. Implement gradient accumulation with accumulation_steps=4 while reducing batch size to 8 to maintain effective batch size of 32. WHY: The validation loss shows high variance (0.5 to 0.94) indicating sensitivity to the split. Using all 5 folds reduces variance and utilizes full data. Gradient accumulation stabilizes gradient updates, as small batches (32) give noisy estimates. Historical experiments show that ensemble averaging and larger effective batch sizes improve generalization. HOW: Loop over all 5 folds (skf.split), for each fold train a separate model, store test predictions, then average. Use GradScaler for mixed precision if desired. Keep early stopping, same loss, optimizer, scheduler. Interface constraints: preserve variable names like `best_model.pt` should become `best_model_fold{i}.pt`; submission must average all folds."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan proposes three changes: multi-fold cross-validation, tokenizer parallelism fixes, and gradient accumulation. Execution results show that validation loss oscillates wildly (e.g., 0.5059 at epoch 10, 0.8604 at epoch 11) and repeated tokenizer warnings occur, confirming the issues. Historical successful experiments used a DeBERTa-v3 transformer with raw text and a single train/val split, achieving reasonable performance but left room for improvement. The failures in historical experiments (e.g., feature engineering with dimension mismatch) reinforce the need for focus on data processing and training strategy. Therefore, refinements should target data_processing_and_feature_engineering to fix tokenizer warnings, training_evaluation to implement multi-fold CV and gradient accumulation, while model_design remains unchanged (DeBERTa-v3 is already correct).",
  "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "WHAT: Add `os.environ['TOKENIZERS_PARALLELISM']='false'` at the top of the script before any tokenizer imports, and move tokenization inside the DataLoader worker initialization by using a custom Dataset that tokenizes in __getitem__ (not in collate_fn) to avoid fork-related deadlocks. WHY: The execution shows repeated tokenizer parallelism warnings, which can cause data loading corruption and silent performance degradation. Historical experiments confirm that stable tokenization is critical for reproducibility. HOW: Insert environment variable setting right after path config and before any transformers import; modify TextDataset to tokenize in __getitem__ and store tokenized outputs, or use a custom collate_fn that tokenizes within the worker process. Interface constraints: preserve existing dataset class name and method signatures, but adapt internal implementation.",
    "training_evaluation": "WHAT: Replace the single 80/20 split with 5-fold StratifiedKFold cross-validation (as already set up but only first fold used) and average predictions from all 5 models. Implement gradient accumulation with accumulation_steps=4 while reducing batch size to 8 to maintain effective batch size of 32. WHY: The validation loss shows high variance (0.5 to 0.94) indicating sensitivity to the split. Using all 5 folds reduces variance and utilizes full data. Gradient accumulation stabilizes gradient updates, as small batches (32) give noisy estimates. Historical experiments show that ensemble averaging and larger effective batch sizes improve generalization. HOW: Loop over all 5 folds (skf.split), for each fold train a separate model, store test predictions, then average. Use GradScaler for mixed precision if desired. Keep early stopping, same loss, optimizer, scheduler. Interface constraints: preserve variable names like `best_model.pt` should become `best_model_fold{i}.pt`; submission must average all folds."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The initial plan aims to reduce variance and overfitting by replacing single hold-out with 5-fold CV, adding attention pooling, text augmentation, and focal loss. Historical experiments show that using DeBERTa-v3-base with cross-validation and proper regularization significantly improves log loss compared to single-split fine-tuning. However, the first historical experiment (which switched to pure transformer) failed to achieve state-of-the-art, suggesting that handcrafted features still provide value. The second historical experiment demonstrated that feature-rich architectures with cross-attention can improve log loss, but the current code already uses handcrafted features. Given the execution results showing high variance and overfitting (val loss spiking from 0.3537 to 0.9144), I will implement the planned changes to training_evaluation and model_design to stabilize training. Data augmentation via synonym replacement and insertion of author-specific words is also added to model_design to address overfitting. Data processing remains largely unchanged because features are well-engineered, but I will add the augmentation logic. Focal loss is integrated into training_evaluation.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Add attention-weighted pooling: replace [CLS] extraction with a learnable attention pooling layer that computes a weighted sum of all token hidden states. Define an AttentionPooling class with an nn.Linear(1024,1) layer; in forward, apply softmax across tokens and compute weighted sum. Also add text-level augmentation: synonym replacement for 10% of non-stop words using WordNet (via nlpaug), and random insertion of author-specific words (from ARCHAIC_WORDS, EMOTIONAL_WORDS, LOVECRAFT_WORDS) at random positions in 15% of training sentences. Integrate augmentation inside the Dataset __getitem__ during DeBERTa training. Keep the same DeBERTa-v3-large backbone and classifier head. This change addresses the insufficient representation from [CLS] and provides regularization through augmentation.", "training_evaluation": "Replace the current 10% hold-out split with 5-fold cross-validation for DeBERTa training. For each fold, train a model on 4/5 of the data and evaluate on the held-out 1/5. After all folds, extract embeddings from each fold's held-out predictions and average the validation and test probability outputs across folds. Use the best model per fold (based on validation loss) for extraction. Keep the same optimizer (AdamW), scheduler, and early stopping (patience=5) but train for up to 15 epochs per fold. For the loss function, replace CrossEntropyLoss with Focal Loss (gamma=2.0) to down-weight easy examples and focus on minority classes. Also adjust label smoothing to 0.1. This change reduces variance, uses all data for training/validation, and directly addresses class imbalance."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan aims to reduce variance and overfitting by replacing single hold-out with 5-fold CV, adding attention pooling, text augmentation, and focal loss. Historical experiments show that using DeBERTa-v3-base with cross-validation and proper regularization significantly improves log loss compared to single-split fine-tuning. However, the first historical experiment (which switched to pure transformer) failed to achieve state-of-the-art, suggesting that handcrafted features still provide value. The second historical experiment demonstrated that feature-rich architectures with cross-attention can improve log loss, but the current code already uses handcrafted features. Given the execution results showing high variance and overfitting (val loss spiking from 0.3537 to 0.9144), I will implement the planned changes to training_evaluation and model_design to stabilize training. Data augmentation via synonym replacement and insertion of author-specific words is also added to model_design to address overfitting. Data processing remains largely unchanged because features are well-engineered, but I will add the augmentation logic. Focal loss is integrated into training_evaluation.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Add attention-weighted pooling: replace [CLS] extraction with a learnable attention pooling layer that computes a weighted sum of all token hidden states. Define an AttentionPooling class with an nn.Linear(1024,1) layer; in forward, apply softmax across tokens and compute weighted sum. Also add text-level augmentation: synonym replacement for 10% of non-stop words using WordNet (via nlpaug), and random insertion of author-specific words (from ARCHAIC_WORDS, EMOTIONAL_WORDS, LOVECRAFT_WORDS) at random positions in 15% of training sentences. Integrate augmentation inside the Dataset __getitem__ during DeBERTa training. Keep the same DeBERTa-v3-large backbone and classifier head. This change addresses the insufficient representation from [CLS] and provides regularization through augmentation.",
    "training_evaluation": "Replace the current 10% hold-out split with 5-fold cross-validation for DeBERTa training. For each fold, train a model on 4/5 of the data and evaluate on the held-out 1/5. After all folds, extract embeddings from each fold's held-out predictions and average the validation and test probability outputs across folds. Use the best model per fold (based on validation loss) for extraction. Keep the same optimizer (AdamW), scheduler, and early stopping (patience=5) but train for up to 15 epochs per fold. For the loss function, replace CrossEntropyLoss with Focal Loss (gamma=2.0) to down-weight easy examples and focus on minority classes. Also adjust label smoothing to 0.1. This change reduces variance, uses all data for training/validation, and directly addresses class imbalance."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution results show clear overfitting: DeBERTa training loss drops to 0.06 while validation loss increases to 0.79, and the ensemble val log loss is 0.2553. The initial plan directly addresses overfitting via multi-sample dropout, cosine annealing with restarts, increased dropout, and SWA. Similar historical experiments succeeded by simplifying to a pure transformer pipeline and focusing on regularization. Therefore, refinements concentrate on model_design to implement multi-sample dropout and increase dropout rate, and training_evaluation to replace the scheduler, add SWA, and adjust early stopping. Data processing is kept unchanged since handcrafted features are already used and historical plans showed that removing them may not be optimal given time constraints.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Modify the DeBERTa model class to add Multi-Sample Dropout (MSD) in the classifier head. Instead of a single dropout, apply dropout K=4 times during training (forward method when training), average the resulting logits, and compute loss on averaged logits. Increase DROPOUT from 0.2 to 0.3. The model design changes require overriding the forward method to loop over dropout masks, accumulating logits, and averaging them. This directly addresses overfitting observed in execution (training loss 0.06, val loss 0.79) by providing a Monte Carlo estimate of the expected output, as successful historical experiments with DeBERTa-v3-large showed improvement with MSD.", "training_evaluation": "Replace the linear warmup scheduler with CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts (T_0=4, T_mult=2). Implement Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) after training: after early stopping triggers, continue training for 5 more epochs with a constant low lr (1e-6) and average the model weights. Keep early stopping patience at 5 but monitor validation log loss. Also, implement the custom loss calculation based on averaged logits from MSD. This change leverages historical success of cosine annealing with restarts to escape overfitted minima and SWA to find flatter minima, improving generalization as indicated by the current overfitting pattern."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results show clear overfitting: DeBERTa training loss drops to 0.06 while validation loss increases to 0.79, and the ensemble val log loss is 0.2553. The initial plan directly addresses overfitting via multi-sample dropout, cosine annealing with restarts, increased dropout, and SWA. Similar historical experiments succeeded by simplifying to a pure transformer pipeline and focusing on regularization. Therefore, refinements concentrate on model_design to implement multi-sample dropout and increase dropout rate, and training_evaluation to replace the scheduler, add SWA, and adjust early stopping. Data processing is kept unchanged since handcrafted features are already used and historical plans showed that removing them may not be optimal given time constraints.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Modify the DeBERTa model class to add Multi-Sample Dropout (MSD) in the classifier head. Instead of a single dropout, apply dropout K=4 times during training (forward method when training), average the resulting logits, and compute loss on averaged logits. Increase DROPOUT from 0.2 to 0.3. The model design changes require overriding the forward method to loop over dropout masks, accumulating logits, and averaging them. This directly addresses overfitting observed in execution (training loss 0.06, val loss 0.79) by providing a Monte Carlo estimate of the expected output, as successful historical experiments with DeBERTa-v3-large showed improvement with MSD.",
    "training_evaluation": "Replace the linear warmup scheduler with CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts (T_0=4, T_mult=2). Implement Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) after training: after early stopping triggers, continue training for 5 more epochs with a constant low lr (1e-6) and average the model weights. Keep early stopping patience at 5 but monitor validation log loss. Also, implement the custom loss calculation based on averaged logits from MSD. This change leverages historical success of cosine annealing with restarts to escape overfitted minima and SWA to find flatter minima, improving generalization as indicated by the current overfitting pattern."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The execution results confirm the initial plan's analysis: severe overfitting in DeBERTa (val loss spikes after epoch 3, best at epoch 3, then diverges; final val log loss 0.8627 is much worse than XGBoost 0.2926). The initial plan's proposed changes (Multi-Sample Dropout, SWA, balanced sampling, increased patience, layer-wise LRs, higher weight decay) are directly targeted to address this overfitting. Historical successful experiments show that feature-rich architectures with transformer fusion can improve log loss from ~0.85 to ~0.70-0.75, while a failed experiment with MSD and cosine annealing worsened overfitting. Therefore, I refine the initial plan by focusing on training_evaluation and model_design, leveraging the MSD and SWA from the initial plan but avoiding aggressive scheduler changes (cosine annealing with restarts failed historically). Instead, I keep the linear scheduler but increase patience and add a cyclic LR with longer period (inspired by successful cosine annealing but with more conservative settings to avoid destabilization). Data processing remains unchanged since features are already well-engineered.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "1) Implement Multi-Sample Dropout (MSD) in the DeBERTa classifier head: during training, perform K=4 forward passes through the classifier with dropout enabled, average logits, then compute loss. During inference, do the same for smoother probabilities. This directly reduces prediction variance and acts as a Bayesian approximation, addressing the overfitting observed (initial plan's change #1, refined: keep K=4 as it worked in similar tasks). 2) Set hidden_dropout_prob and attention_probs_dropout_prob to 0.3 (increased from 0.2) to further regularize. 3) Preserve the model structure and forward interface: the model should accept input_ids and attention_mask, and return logits (or averaged logits when training with MSD). The forward method will loop over dropout masks only during training; during eval it defaults to standard dropout.", "training_evaluation": "1) Implement layer-wise adaptive learning rates: set lr=2e-5 for last 6 transformer layers and classifier head, lr=5e-6 for middle 12 layers, lr=1e-6 for first 6 layers and embedding layer (initial plan's change #3). 2) Increase weight decay to 0.05 (from 0.01) to penalize large updates. 3) Implement balanced sampling via WeightedRandomSampler to oversample minority class MWS (initial plan's change #2). 4) Increase patience from 5 to 10 to allow escaping the sharp minimum at epoch 3 (initial plan's change #2). 5) Implement Stochastic Weight Averaging (SWA) after early stopping: continue training for 5 epochs with constant low lr=1e-6 and average model weights (initial plan's change #1, refined: keep SWA but use constant low lr instead of cosine annealing, as the latter failed historically). 6) Keep linear scheduler with warmup (WARMUP_RATIO=0.1) to avoid destabilization seen in failed experiment with cosine restarts. 7) Preserve all other training parameters (batch size, gradient accumulation, label smoothing, AMP) and the evaluation function for log loss and accuracy."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results confirm the initial plan's analysis: severe overfitting in DeBERTa (val loss spikes after epoch 3, best at epoch 3, then diverges; final val log loss 0.8627 is much worse than XGBoost 0.2926). The initial plan's proposed changes (Multi-Sample Dropout, SWA, balanced sampling, increased patience, layer-wise LRs, higher weight decay) are directly targeted to address this overfitting. Historical successful experiments show that feature-rich architectures with transformer fusion can improve log loss from ~0.85 to ~0.70-0.75, while a failed experiment with MSD and cosine annealing worsened overfitting. Therefore, I refine the initial plan by focusing on training_evaluation and model_design, leveraging the MSD and SWA from the initial plan but avoiding aggressive scheduler changes (cosine annealing with restarts failed historically). Instead, I keep the linear scheduler but increase patience and add a cyclic LR with longer period (inspired by successful cosine annealing but with more conservative settings to avoid destabilization). Data processing remains unchanged since features are already well-engineered.",
